Done. Wrote `qwen35_fast/gemv.py` (200 lines, `py_compile` + `pyflakes` clean).

Highlights:
- `_gemv_kernel`: N-blocked, K-streamed kernel using `tl.dot` (bf16→fp32), W read exactly once, coalesced along K; constexpr `SPLIT_K` path writes fp32 partials.
- `_reduce_kernel`: small fp32 split-K reduction (only launched when `SPLIT_K>1`).
- Static `_BASE`/`_TABLE` config keyed by `(M,K,N)` for all listed shapes, with a heuristic fallback; no runtime autotune.
- `gemv(x, W)`: no host syncs, no data-dependent Python, workspace preallocated and cached by `(M,K,N,split,device)` → CUDA-graph-safe.
- `test()` checks all shapes × M=1..4 vs `torch.matmul` with a 2-bf16-ulp tolerance; `bench(autotune=True)` sweeps configs and prints best GB/s; `bench()` reports ours vs torch.

No GPU here, so `test()`/`bench()` are unrun; only static checks were possible. No other files touched.
